## Science references

- [NASA — International Space Station Facts and Figures](https://www.nasa.gov/international-space-station/space-station-facts-and-figures/): approximately 90-minute orbits; about 16 sunrises/sunsets daily; exercise for at least two hours; international agency partnership; continuous occupation since November 2000.
- [NASA — What Is Microgravity?](https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/glenn/what-is-microgravity/): gravity remains strong at ISS altitude; station, crew, and objects fall together; sufficient sideways speed produces continuous free fall around Earth; approximately 28,000 km/h orbital speed.
- [NASA — Observing Our Planet from Low Earth Orbit](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/station/iss-research/observing-our-planet-from-low-earth-orbit/): approximately 400 km altitude and 90-minute period. Height and period vary; the reader labels them as approximate.
- [NASA — Why Do Research on the International Space Station?](https://www.nasa.gov/missions/station/iss-research/why-do-research-on-the-international-space-station/): sustained microgravity, exposure to space, Earth observations, and research applications.
- [NASA — So You Want to Be a Space Farmer…](https://www.nasa.gov/humans-in-space/so-you-want-to-be-a-space-farmer/): growing plants aboard the ISS and learning to support future missions.
- The specific Veggie image record above confirms that the photographed plants were part of a real ISS investigation, rather than a ground demonstration or a decorative illustration.

## Teaching illustrations and editorial choices

`assets/orbit.svg` is an original schematic based on NASA's microgravity explanation. The ISS symbol is at the top of a circular orbit centered on Earth. The forward-motion arrow is tangent to that path; the gravity arrow points inward toward Earth's center. The dashed curve is the orbit. Distances, spacecraft sizes, and arrow lengths are not to scale. It is labeled as a teaching diagram and distinguished from NASA photographs.

The five vocabulary SVGs are original explanatory drawings, not photographs. Their meanings are also written as selectable text. The print edition draws the same vector illustrations.

The revised globe uses [Natural Earth 1:110m land data](https://github.com/nvkelso/natural-earth-vector/blob/master/geojson/ne_110m_land.geojson), available in the [public domain](https://www.naturalearthdata.com/about/terms-of-use/). Its coastlines are drawn with an orthographic projection centered on 20° W, 12° N. The source GeoJSON and generated projection paths are retained in `tools/data/`. D3 Geo is used only for authoring the retained paths; the deployed diagram is a self-contained SVG. The ISS silhouette is a simplified teaching illustration, not an engineering configuration drawing.

The supplied manuscript is retained as `MANUSCRIPT.md` in the working folder. Its four-section narrative is the editorial source. Short paragraphs were combined for coherent translation blocks; the print text was edited for layout. The fixed statement about continuous occupation since November 2000 avoids a time-dependent anniversary count. Everyday care and exercise details are retained in the reading and supporting boxes.

The European Space Agency represents multiple European countries; Europe is not described as a single country. Microgravity is not explained as the absence of Earth's gravity. Approximate orbital figures are not presented as an exact daily flight schedule.

NASA and other credited agencies do not endorse this independently prepared educational reader.
